.TH tudhash n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tudhash
.PP
A perceptual \fBdifference hash\fR (dHash) in pure Tcl, Tk\-free. dHash turns an image into a 64\-bit fingerprint so that visually similar images get similar fingerprints: two pictures are "the same" when the \fBHamming distance\fR between their hashes is small. This finds near\-duplicate scans/photos (rescaled, recompressed, slightly adjusted) that an exact byte hash (\fBsha256\fR, \fBxxhash\fR) would never match.
.PP
Pure Tcl, 8.6+ / 9.x. The bit convention matches the common dHash (e.g. python \fBimagehash.dhash\fR), verified against it.
.SS Commands
.CS
tudhash::fromGray w h grayList         ;# -> 16 hex chars (64-bit hash)
tudhash::fromRGB  w h rgbList          ;# -> 16 hex chars
tudhash::distance hexA hexB            ;# -> 0..64 (Hamming distance)
tudhash::similar  hexA hexB ?maxDist?  ;# -> bool (default maxDist 10)
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBgrayList\fR: \fBw*h\fR integers 0..255, row\-major.
.IP \(bu
\fBrgbList\fR: \fBw*h*3\fR integers 0..255, row\-major (\fBr g b r g b ...\fR); reduced to luminance internally.
.PP
The image may be any size — it is box\-averaged down to 9×8 and each pixel is compared with its right neighbour, row by row, giving 8×8 = 64 bits.
.SS "This is the Tk\-free core"
.PP
\fBtudhash\fR deliberately works on a \fBpixel grid\fR, not on image files. Decoding an image into that grid is a separate, format\-dependent step. Keeping it out of \fBtclutils\fR is what lets this module stay Tk\-free. Get the grid however suits the caller:
.PP
\fBWith Tk (photo + the \fR\fBImg\fR\fB package for JPEG/TIFF/…):\fR best for a GUI or a client that already has Tk. Belongs in a \fBtkutils\fR app, not here:
.CS
package require Tk
# package require Img   ;# for JPEG/TIFF/etc.
proc imageToGray {path} {
    set img [image create photo -file $path]
    set w [image width $img]; set h [image height $img]
    set gray {}
    for {set y 0} {$y < $h} {incr y} {
        for {set x 0} {$x < $w} {incr x} {
            lassign [$img get $x $y] r g b
            lappend gray [expr {(77*$r + 150*$g + 29*$b) >> 8}]
        }
    }
    image delete $img
    return [list $w $h $gray]
}
lassign [imageToGray scan.png] w h gray
set hash [tudhash::fromGray $w $h $gray]
.CE
.PP
\fBWith an external tool\fR (headless server, no Tk): let \fBconvert\fR (ImageMagick) or \fBpnmscale\fR produce a small grayscale PGM/raw and read the bytes. Any route that yields \fBw\fR, \fBh\fR and the gray values works.
.SS Example
.CS
package require tclutils::tudhash
namespace import ::tclutils::tudhash::*

set a [fromGray $w1 $h1 $gray1]
set b [fromGray $w2 $h2 $gray2]
if {[similar $a $b]} { puts "near-duplicate (distance [distance $a $b])" }
.CE
.PP
Typical thresholds on the 64\-bit hash: \fB0\fR identical reduction, \fB≤ 5\fR almost certainly the same picture, \fB≤ 10\fR likely related, \fB> 15\fR different. Tune \fBmaxDist\fR to the corpus.
.SS "dHash vs. exact hashes"
.PP
\fBxxhash\fR/\fBsha256\fR answer "are these the exact same bytes"; \fBtudhash\fR answers "do these look like the same image". A re\-saved JPEG has a different \fBsha256\fR but the same (or a very close) dHash. Use the byte hash first for exact dedup, then \fBtudhash\fR to catch the visual near\-duplicates that survived.
.SS Errors
.PP
Error code \fB{TCLUTILS TUDHASH <REASON>}\fR:
.TS
l l.
REASON	When
\fBDIM\fR	width/height not positive integers
\fBDATA\fR	grayList/rgbList has the wrong number of values
\fBHASH\fR	a hash passed to \fBdistance\fR/\fBsimilar\fR is not 16 hex chars
.TE
.SS Testing
.PP
\fBtests/tudhash.test\fR checks the bit convention against a real 9×8 reduction from \fBimagehash\fR, the all\-ones/all\-zeros gradients, the distance/similar helpers, the RGB→gray path and the down\-scaler. Set \fBTCLUTILS_TM\fR and run with \fBtclsh\fR (passes on 8.6 and 9.0).
